WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:06.299
Film Courage: Have you ever written a screenplay
where the intention was not to film it yourself?

00:00:06.299 --> 00:00:08.880
Just write it for a director for hire to make?

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:16.540
Mark Harris, Writer/Director: Yes, my first
ten screenplays was written other for directors

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:21.570
to direct because I had no desire to direct
films at all.

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:26.929
It wasn’t until I did WHY MEN CHEAT and
then everything after that is for me to I’m

00:00:26.929 --> 00:00:29.769
going to direct this stuff.

00:00:29.769 --> 00:00:38.660
But everything that was written prior to me
directing my first feature was for other directors

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:44.640
to direct because I never had a desire to
ever, ever direct a film until I actually

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:50.040
go on the set for the first time and I was
like “Oh, this is what I’m passionate

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:51.040
about.”

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:57.550
Film Courage: Were you nervous or you just
didn’t want to have that responsibility

00:00:57.550 --> 00:01:01.190
of directing and managing 50 things at once.

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:09.220
Mark: Well when I first got on a film set
days leading up to directing I was definitely

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:16.720
nervous but it wasn’t because I didn’t
want to manage but it was because I didn’t

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:22.340
have the confidence because I didn’t know
what I was doing so there was that insecurity

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:26.550
of not knowing what I was doing.

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:29.630
So we had all of those things going on.

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:36.320
I was insecure, I didn’t have the confidence
that I have now because I didn’t know what

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:38.270
I was doing.

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:50.850
But this has been quite a few films whereas
it took awhile to get the confidence for me

00:01:50.850 --> 00:02:00.080
to deal with actors, to deal with…to know
how to set up…to do my shot list and everything.

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:02.810
So this was over a period of time.

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:04.570
I didn’t just direct my first film.

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:08.619
Then my second film, I knew it.

00:02:08.619 --> 00:02:12.260
Again for me it was like going though film
school, every project is going through film

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:14.020
school.

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:24.170
But as I was learning I was building my confidence
up at the same time because again if you don’t

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:29.250
know what you’re doing you’re insecure
about what you’re doing.

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:36.420
And I wasn’t secure as far as being a director
because I didn’t have the knowledge of what

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:37.420
I was doing.

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:40.570
Film Courage: I would imagine you were secure
with sports?

00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:45.209
You said earlier in the interview you played
baseball and basketball at this local park

00:02:45.209 --> 00:02:46.370
[near Chicago, IL] or something?

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:50.959
So could you take this same feeling of confidence
to this?

00:02:50.959 --> 00:02:56.110
In some ways it’s similar, you’re in a
big arena, you’re playing with a bunch of

00:02:56.110 --> 00:02:57.110
people.

00:02:57.110 --> 00:03:01.569
Some are on your team, maybe some aren’t
and you’re kind of moving around.

00:03:01.569 --> 00:03:02.920
There is a lot of moving pieces

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:12.130
Mark: Yes but as it relates to sports because
I played Little League Baseball which I wasn’t

00:03:12.130 --> 00:03:15.319
very good at Baseball.

00:03:15.319 --> 00:03:23.780
So when I played once (I was a Cubs fan),
I love Shawn Donston, he was the short stop

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:30.960
for the Chicago Cubs and I wanted to play
short stop because I was a Shawon Dunston

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:33.640
fan.

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:37.400
So when I got to Little League, I wanted to
play.

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:40.360
They would hit the ball and I’d be “I
don’t know about this?”

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:44.140
There was still that insecurity because I
wasn’t a good baseball player.

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:45.140
Now basketball was different.

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:48.120
I was pretty good at basketball and I also
wrestled.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:53.230
I was a wrestler in high school so I was pretty
good at wrestling.

00:03:53.230 --> 00:04:00.500
But even when I started wrestling and I was
losing I had to get that confidence to know

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:06.880
that I could win so when I came to my second
year…so this is what my coach did (my wrestling

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:08.190
coach).

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:13.510
My first year I didn’t go down state because
it was my first year wrestling so I was losing

00:04:13.510 --> 00:04:22.719
a lot but he took me down state with the other
city champions to help me as a wrestler but

00:04:22.719 --> 00:04:26.360
that built my confidence up and I was like
“I can do this.”

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:30.800
The next year I placed in City and the year
after that I placed in City, so the next two

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:32.639
years I went down state.

00:04:32.639 --> 00:04:37.400
But I had to have my confidence built.

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:45.069
As a filmmaker when I first hit that set I
wasn’t sure who I was as a filmmaker because

00:04:45.069 --> 00:04:53.610
there was that nervousness but I’m like
“I’m going to do it.”

00:04:53.610 --> 00:05:02.729
I wasn’t afraid, I overcame that fear, but
I just needed more confidence because I lacked

00:05:02.729 --> 00:05:03.729
the knowledge.

00:05:03.729 --> 00:05:08.680
But once I started to go and do it, and do
it more, and do it more, and do it more, it

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:15.020
just gave me an example you know when a father
tells his son to jump off of a porch so he

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:21.190
can catch him and his son is hesitant at first
so he does it one time.

00:05:21.190 --> 00:05:24.950
Then the second time it becomes so natural
for him to do it, so easy for him to do it

00:05:24.950 --> 00:05:28.999
because he has that confidence that his father
is going to catch him.

00:05:28.999 --> 00:05:29.999
It was the same for me.

00:05:29.999 --> 00:05:32.789
Once I did it one time “Okay…this is…I
like this.”

00:05:32.789 --> 00:05:37.780
Then the second time, the third time, the
fourth time, with each time I was building

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:41.120
my confidence because I was learning more.

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:45.619
I was open to again the constructive criticism.

00:05:45.619 --> 00:05:51.009
I was open to working with and collaborating
and listening to people who knew more than

00:05:51.009 --> 00:05:57.669
I knew and I had to put the ego aside and
say “Okay, I’m going to learn.

00:05:57.669 --> 00:05:59.339
This is what I want to do.”

00:05:59.339 --> 00:06:03.839
So I didn’t go on this film set with pride
and ego.

00:06:03.839 --> 00:06:10.210
I went on there with I knew this was something
I loved and something that I wanted to learn.

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:15.159
So I had put all of that aside and I had to
overcome that fear.

00:06:15.159 --> 00:06:17.689
But that confidence, I had to build that confidence.

